    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "38585271_298cb51c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123361
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-09T00:50:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Originally the interrupt triggered the S0ix entry and exit. But during S0 idle state, the SLP_S0 signal toggles and this causes EC to go in and out of S0ix.\n\nTo avoid this, we introduced the host command where the EC sleeps when the host sleeps and EC wakes when the host wakes.\n\nThe powerindebug command was the starting motivation for a new round of changes. The larger motivation was to make EC enter S0ix when both conditions are met:\n\u003e\u003e host has slept\n\u003e\u003e SLP_S0 signal has toggled\n\nwhich means EC waits for both the host command and the signal.\n\nNow coming to exit. We can have two approaches here:\n\u003e\u003e EC exits just on the signal or \n\u003e\u003e EC exits on both the signal and the host command.\n\nWe went with the first option but we can go with the second one as well to make both entry and exit approaches consistent.\n\nThe signal detect patches are an additional option. If EC is waiting for the signal, it might as well wake up the host if the signal does not come within the specified interval. This will prevent error conditions where kernel is sleeping but the system hasn\u0027t entered S0ix yet. There is a kernel based mechanism for this from Derek as well.\n\nWhen we wake up the system on this error and lid happens to be closed (because lid close triggered the sleep chain), the system will go back to sleep. So one has to force the lid open before forcefully waking up the system.\n\nSo there are two sets of changes:\n\u003e\u003e A change to the EC S0ix transition trigger - it now will depend on both the host command and interrupt.\n\u003e\u003e An additional SLP_S0 signal assertion failure detection which will wake up the system.",
      "parentUuid": "a68fa037_473fd910",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "0467a7e38411a7ca6409d180030bd2aa0d422984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6680d456_a73e18c9",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-09T02:44:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \n\u003e The signal detect patches are an additional option. If EC is waiting for the signal, it might as well wake up the host if the signal does not come within the specified interval. This will prevent error conditions where kernel is sleeping but the system hasn\u0027t entered S0ix yet. There is a kernel based mechanism for this from Derek as well.\n\nIf we have a kernel mechanism for this why are we messing around with the EC?\n\n\u003e \n\u003e When we wake up the system on this error and lid happens to be closed (because lid close triggered the sleep chain), the system will go back to sleep. So one has to force the lid open before forcefully waking up the system.\n\nWhy does this error happen? And what interval are you waiting for slp_s0 to assert? Either the lid is open or it isn\u0027t. It sounds like you are losing a race with waking, but it\u0027s not really clear the sequence of events you are referring to..",
      "parentUuid": "38585271_298cb51c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "0467a7e38411a7ca6409d180030bd2aa0d422984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e0d26bf3_76b1a303",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123361
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-09T03:44:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the signal detection option in EC is not needed, then we don\u0027t have to merge patches #2 and 3. That was the reason why they were raised as separate patches.\n\nThe kernel mechanism is an out of upstream tree patch maintained only in Chromium git - the patch keeps a timer alive after the kernel sleeps to wake up and check if residency counters were incremented. EC seems easier and more straight forward - the signal is wired to the EC and EC can check if it was raised or not.\n\nComing back to forced lid open. Here is the sequence:\n\nS0ix is enabled in the system\nLid is closed\nSystem goes to sleep (S0ix)\nSLP_S0 fails to assert\nSystem is woken up (either by EC or by kernel mechanisms)\nSystem will go back to sleep since lid is closed (unless this is handled by userland)\n\nSo the lid is forced open. If userland can fix this behavior, then forcing lid open is not needed.\n\nSLP_S0 signal assertion failure is not normal. We will be debugging all known reasons for this. But in SKL, we felt we needed a mechanism to wake up the system if it ever encounters this scenario.\n\nTo summarize: there are two patchsets here.\n\n#1 to make EC depend on both host command and SLP_S0 signal for entering S0ix\n#2 an optional SLP_S0 signal assertion failure mechanism in EC.",
      "parentUuid": "6680d456_a73e18c9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "0467a7e38411a7ca6409d180030bd2aa0d422984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "adb32681_d18e577d",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-09T04:04:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e S0ix is enabled in the system\n\u003e Lid is closed\n\u003e System goes to sleep (S0ix)\n\u003e SLP_S0 fails to assert\n\u003e System is woken up (either by EC or by kernel mechanisms)\n\u003e System will go back to sleep since lid is closed (unless this is handled by userland)\n\nIn the last step, isn\u0027t it the right thing to do i.e. since lid is closed, system should go back to sleep. Why would we want to force lid open using a virtual switch and keep the system awake?\n\nIIUC, since lid is closed, we would want to suspend the system and save power. If for some reason entry to S0ix failed, it would be better for the system to try to suspend again in the hope that the next suspend will be successful.",
      "parentUuid": "e0d26bf3_76b1a303",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "0467a7e38411a7ca6409d180030bd2aa0d422984",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c241210a_e33aacf3",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123361
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-09T04:08:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e IIUC, since lid is closed, we would want to suspend the system and save power. If for some reason entry to S0ix failed, it would be better for the system to try to suspend again in the hope that the next suspend will be successful.\n\nIf the signal again fails, it will again be woken up, it will again go back to sleep because lid is closed and it will be a loop.",
      "parentUuid": "adb32681_d18e577d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "0467a7e38411a7ca6409d180030bd2aa0d422984",
